        "The paper argues that the institutional blindness of these approaches can largely be explained by a fundamental feature shared by these seemingly disparate frameworks. While these conceptual approaches have important differences among them, the one unifying thread that ties them together is that they are all variants of a society-based approach. The semi-colonial, semi-feudal class approach, Migdalian strong society-weak state approach, Weberian neopatrimonial approach, and different versions of hybrid approaches are all society-based approaches because state policies are explained by the configuration of social forces (whether of class, society, elites, interest groups, social movements, or any other society-based actors). Political institutions, if at all visible, are treated as epiphenomena of autonomous social actors. Studying the nature of these institutions is not considered worthy of sustained critical focus and analysis.",
